    {
      "id": "uc-003",
      "name_en": "Benefits Eligibility Triage",
      "name_ar": "تحديد أهلية الاستفادة من المزايا الاجتماعية",
      "description_en": "An AI system that assists in triaging citizen applications for government benefits and social support programmes, producing a recommendation that is reviewed by a human case officer before any determination is communicated to the citizen.",
      "description_ar": "نظام ذكاء اصطناعي يساعد في فرز طلبات المواطنين للحصول على المزايا الحكومية وبرامج الدعم الاجتماعي، وينتج توصيةً يراجعها مسؤول بشري قبل إيصال أي تحديد إلى المواطن.",
      "use_case_class": "benefits_triage",
      "confidence_threshold": 0.98,
      "weighting_rationale": "Benefits eligibility triage carries the highest fairness burden of all five use cases. An AI system that exhibits demographic bias here systematically denies or incorrectly grants benefits to citizens on the basis of protected characteristics, constituting both a legal violation under the PDPL and a fundamental injustice. Fairness controls therefore carry the largest single mandatory weight block. Human oversight is weighted second and declared mandatory in full (including override capability) because the UAE AI Ethics Guide requires human-in-the-loop for high-risk decisions; no adverse eligibility determination may be made by the AI alone. Privacy is the third block: income, health, family status, and national identification data are processed, placing this squarely in the sensitive-data category under PDPL Article 3. Explainability controls on decision factors are mandatory because citizens have a right to understand why their application received a particular recommendation, linking to both PDPL Article 12-17 data subject rights and the transparency principle. The threshold of 0.98 is the highest alongside uc-001 because the harms from a false-certified model (discriminatory benefit denial at scale) are severe and difficult to reverse.",
