      "name_en": "Traffic Incident Classification",
      "name_ar": "تصنيف حوادث المرور",
      "description_en": "An AI system that classifies traffic incidents from sensor data and operator reports to prioritise emergency response resource allocation. Classifications are reviewed by a control-room operator before resource dispatch.",
      "description_ar": "نظام ذكاء اصطناعي يصنّف حوادث المرور استناداً إلى بيانات أجهزة الاستشعار وتقارير المشغّلين لترتيب أولويات تخصيص موارد الاستجابة الطارئة. تُراجَع التصنيفات من قِبل مشغّل غرفة التحكم قبل تنسيب الموارد.",
      "use_case_class": "incident_classification",
      "confidence_threshold": 0.95,
      "weighting_rationale": "Traffic incident classification is an operational system feeding emergency response. The primary ethical risk is not demographic fairness (the classification is based on incident characteristics such as location, sensor readings, and report content, not on demographic data about individuals) but accuracy: a misclassified major accident as a minor one could delay a life-saving response. Accuracy controls therefore carry the largest mandatory weight. Human oversight is mandatory because control-room operators must always be in the decision loop before resources are dispatched; the AI's role is to rank and sort, not to act autonomously. Security controls (prompt injection and adversarial stability) are mandatory because the classification system ingests free-text operator reports that could be manipulated. Fairness controls are advisory because the classification input is incident data rather than personal characteristics; they are included to catch any latent bias in location-based or report-text-based classification. The threshold of 0.95 is lower than benefits triage or the citizen chatbot because the professional operator review is the default human-in-the-loop rather than an exception, and because classification speed matters in emergency contexts.",
